title: Genesis 12 – The Call of Abram
description: A study in Genesis 12: God calls Abram and makes him promises, Abram calls on the name of the LORD, and his lapse of faith in Egypt.
output: lessons/genesis/genesis-12-the-call-of-abram.html

=== Introduction
--- The Call of Abram [title]
overline: A study in Genesis 12
sub: The promises, the altar, and a lapse of faith in Egypt.
refs: Genesis 12:1-3
hint: Use the arrow keys or swipe to move. Tap any reference to read the verse.

--- All of Genesis was prologue
lead: All of Genesis, as important as the stories of Adam, Cain, Noah, the flood and Babel are, was prologue to this: the story of Abram and Sarai. Abraham and Sarah.
p: Up to this point, mankind who called upon the name of the {Lord} had the promise of His blessing and protection, of God being their Saviour, but not the specific promise of the man Christ Jesus. Born of man. Seed of Abraham. [[Genesis 4:26]] [[Galatians 3:16]]

=== The promises
--- Get thee out of thy country
scripture: Genesis 12:1-3
- I will make of thee a great nation;
- I will bless thee, and make thy name great; and thou shalt be a blessing:
- I will bless them that bless thee, and curse him that curseth thee:
- and in thee shall all families of the earth be blessed.

--- A promise to Abram himself
lead: These are direct blessings and promises to Abram himself.
panel-warn: Not to the modern state | Many take the personal promise to Abraham, to bless those that bless him and curse those that curse him, and claim it’s an eternal promise to the modern Jew and the apostate state calling itself Israel. This is found nowhere in scripture.
panel: God’s protection of Abram | This is God’s promise to protect him and his wife, as God has called Abraham to go to a different land, to Canaan, and there He would establish his legacy.
center: “In thee shall all families of the earth be blessed” is fulfilled in Christ. [[Galatians 3:8]] [[Acts 3:25-26]]

--- They went forth
scripture: Genesis 12:4-6 | mark: And the Canaanite was then in the land
stat: 75 | years old, and childless
p: This promise was made to a 75 year old, childless Abram, in a place where *the Canaanite was then in the land*. His altar built between the Mediterranean and Jordan, between Galilee and Jerusalem.

--- Unto thy seed will I give this land
scripture: Genesis 12:7-8 | mark: Unto thy seed will I give this land; called upon the name of the LORD
callout: **He was saved by faith through grace.** Note the timing of when that was. It comes up later in scripture. [[Romans 4:9-11]] [[lesson:genesis-17-circumcision|Genesis 17]]

--- The Lord appeared unto Abram
heading: The {Lord} appeared unto Abram
big: John 1:18
p: The {Lord} appearing to Abraham is considered by many to be an Old Testament appearance of Jesus, a theophany. As the Bible says no man hath seen God at any time, many consider the appearance of God unto man in the Old Testament a representation of Christ, who was from the beginning with God and is God. [[John 8:56-58]] [[John 1:1-2]]

=== Into Egypt
--- A famine in the land
scripture: Genesis 12:10 | mark: went down into Egypt to sojourn there
p: A familiar story: a famine in the land, going to Egypt to sojourn, Pharaoh and plagues. This theme of finding refuge and sojourning in Egypt is a picture of young Jesus being taken there by Joseph, just as Jesus’ stepfather’s namesake will. [[Matthew 2:13-15]] [[Genesis 46:3-4]]

--- Say, I pray thee, thou art my sister
scripture: Genesis 12:11-16
p: There in Egypt, worried that Sarai, his 65 year old wife, who was still apparently very fair to look upon, would be taken from him and that he would be killed, Abram tells her to lie and say they are siblings. [[Genesis 17:17]]

--- Plagues on Pharaoh
scripture: Genesis 12:17-20 | mark: the LORD plagued Pharaoh and his house with great plagues
p: This seems to be a lack of faith by Abram, and causes enmity with Pharaoh, who indeed was going to take Sarai, but now, subject to plagues by the {Lord}, angrily sends them out of the shelter of his kingdom.
callout-honest: God cursed those who would curse Abram, despite his lapse of faith. God made that promise to him, and if he trusted God, he wouldn’t fear for his life. As it stood, God came through anyway, but Abram and all his household were sent away, back to Canaan and the famine.

--- Like Abram, we doubt
lead: God makes promises to us who believe: to be blessed, to be heirs, to be a blessing to others, to be protected by Him in faith and saved from death, in the imputed righteousness of Jesus. By His grace and mercy.
- Unfortunately, like Abram, a man of great faith, many of us have doubts. We try to arrange matters and control circumstances out of fear. Sometimes even with convenient lies.
- Abram, who by faith moved his entire house to a strange land at the behest of God, was never told to go to Egypt in the first place. Let alone to lie to save his own skin.
- We should not fear famines or tribulations or even persecution of unregenerate man. We should be faithful unto death, convinced of the hope that we shall be delivered. [[Revelation 2:10]]
center: Even Abram, the father of the seed that contained Christ incarnate, doubted and was weak. But his faith grew stronger the more he walked with God.

=== Abraham believed God
--- By faith Abraham obeyed
big: Hebrews 11:8
scripture: James 2:23 | mark: he was called the Friend of God
refs: 2 Chronicles 20:7

--- Believed and faith
lead: Abraham believed. This is the verb form of faith, the noun, the thing Abraham had. He believed, having faith in God’s word, His promise. And that faith was imputed unto him for righteousness, and he was called *the Friend of God*.
p: If we believe, an action, if we have faith, the evidence of things unseen, a thing to possess, in the word of God, His promises, His testimony, we have His righteousness imputed on us. Not our own righteousness. Not our works. But our faith, which comes from believing, which comes from the word of God. [[Hebrews 11:1]] [[Romans 10:17]]
center: O, to be called the friend of God for our faith!

--- Not of works
scripture: Romans 4:1-5 | mark: Abraham believed God, and it was counted unto him for righteousness
refs: Romans 4:9-12; Romans 4:20-25

--- Faith alone [title]
overline: Genesis 12
heading: Salvation is by faith alone
p: Salvation has always been by hearing the word of God and believing it, which gives us faith to call upon the name of the {Lord}, for which God grants us His grace. Salvation is by faith alone. Yesterday, today and for ever, amen. [[Hebrews 13:8]] [[Romans 10:13-14]]

=== Close
--- The father of the faithful [title]
overline: Genesis 12
point: The promises | A great nation, a great name, and in him all families blessed. In Christ.
point: He called on the name of the Lord | Saved by grace through faith, long before circumcision.
point: A lapse in Egypt | Fear and a convenient lie. God was faithful anyway.
point: The Friend of God | Abraham believed God, and it was counted unto him for righteousness.

